  "prompt": "Evaluate $$\\int_0^1\\frac{x^4(1-x)^2}{1+x^2}\\,dx$$",
  "image": null,
  "choices": null,
  "answer": "7/10-ln(2)",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "answerDisplay": "$\\frac7{10}-\\ln2$",
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Solution",
    "body": "Polynomial division: $x^6-2x^5+x^4=(x^2+1)(x^4-2x^3+2x-2)+2x^2-2x+2$, and $\\frac{2x^2-2x+2}{x^2+1}=2-\\frac{2x}{x^2+1}$. So the integrand is $x^4-2x^3+2x-\\frac{2x}{1+x^2}$ and the integral is $\\frac15-\\frac12+1-\\ln2=\\frac7{10}-\\ln2$."
   }
  ],
